      <h1 class="mb-8">Precision Illusion: A Framework for Studying Unsupported Numerical Specificity in AI</h1>
      <div class="prose-page text-base leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-text)] space-y-4">
        <p>AI outputs can contain precise numbers that are not adequately supported by the available evidence — for example, citing “a 38.7% reduction” without a traceable source. We call this communication failure the <strong>precision illusion</strong>.</p>
        <p>The precision illusion is a <strong>research construct, not a measured prevalence rate</strong>. It describes cases where an AI output expresses numerical specificity that cannot be justified from verifiable evidence.</p>
        <p>Why does this matter? Research on the precision heuristic suggests that precise numbers can be perceived as more credible than less precise estimates, even when that precision is unwarranted. Unsupported numerical specificity may therefore influence reliance even when an answer is directionally plausible.</p>
        <p>Our current work focuses on three questions:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Operationalization:</strong> How should unsupported numerical specificity be reliably detected and classified? The UCS taxonomy is our current proposal.</li>
          <li><strong>Measurement:</strong> How does it vary across domains, models, and interaction contexts? Protocol-valid prevalence estimates are not yet established.</li>
          <li><strong>Intervention:</strong> Can these outputs be detected and repaired without degrading appropriate outputs? CheckMyCoach is an ongoing research prototype.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>We are building toward controlled human experiments that test whether unsupported numerical specificity affects trust and reliance, and whether correction changes user behavior.</p>
        <p><em>Status note: Earlier exploratory runs produced preliminary descriptive numbers, but those are not treated as protocol-valid prevalence estimates. Current work focuses on reproducible measurement and behavioral validation.</em></p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
